# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env carries the gateway key. .env.example is the template and IS committed.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
litellm/.env

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.venv/
venv/
.Python
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/

# Tool caches
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
*.tsbuildinfo
.vite/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated data
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The seed pack is reproducible from scripts/generate_data.py, so it is built
# rather than carried. Everything below is an output of running the system.
#
# `data/profiles/` is the exception and is deliberately NOT ignored: a retailer
# profile is the *input* the pack is generated from - the assortment, the
# supplier roster, which branches are regulated - and losing it would mean the
# generator had nothing to build. The pattern below is `data/*.json`, which
# matches the generated catalog at the top level and not the profiles beneath
# it; do not widen it to `data/**/*.json`.
data/*.json
data/*.jsonl
data/*.npy
data/comms/
data/docs/
data/media/
# The extraction answer key is written by the same generator, out of the same
# payloads, so it is rebuilt rather than carried - that is the whole reason it
# cannot rot. The eval's own output goes beside it; the numbers worth keeping
# are the ones recorded in the README.
data/golden/
data/eval/
# What suppliers have sent through the vendor intake, and the lines a reviewer
# has accepted from their proposals. Both are things this installation did, not
# things the repository describes.
data/inbox/

# The supplier data pack. Built by scripts/build_datapack.py from the catalog
# and the retailer profile, so it is derived twice over and belongs in the
# repository no more than the seed pack it is derived from. A committed pack
# would also be the one copy of the schema that does not move when an
# attribute is added, which is the drift the generator exists to prevent.
data/datapack/

# Neo4j, unpacked by `startup.bat graph`. A hundred and fifty megabytes of
# somebody else's database, and `scripts/get_neo4j.py` puts it back in one
# command - so it is generated output like `data/`, not source.
neo4j/
# The archive it unpacks from, kept so a second machine or a re-run does not
# re-download it.
.cache/

# Databases: the application store, the LangGraph checkpoints beside it, and
# the SQLite sidecar files that appear in WAL mode.
data/*.db
data/*.db-wal
data/*.db-shm

# Per-module test artefacts. conftest cleans these up on a clean exit; a run
# killed halfway leaves them behind, and they are worthless either way.
data/test_*.env

# The authored corpus lives in corpus/ and IS committed - standards, channel
# specs, policies and prior incidents are content, not generated data. Only the
# operational seed pack is ignored.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
logs/
*.log

# LangGraph Studio's dev server scratch. Regenerated on every `startup.bat
# studio`, and it carries absolute paths from whoever ran it last.
.langgraph_api/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The built frontend is committed ON PURPOSE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The lab must be able to run this without npm, so frontend/dist ships in the
# repository. Nothing above ignores it - this negation exists because `dist/`
# is in most Python and Node templates and someone will eventually paste one
# in. The `/**` matters: git will not re-include a file whose parent directory
# was excluded, so negating only the directory would not be enough.
!frontend/dist/
!frontend/dist/**

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors and operating systems
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
